A||N||5||Heisenhund||R134 conversion questions||||5||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||04-11-2001||12:24 AM||||OK, the long term benefits of R134 have made me decide to do the swap. Since all my R12 oozed out during the engine rebuild (when the Compressor was sitting on a shelf in my garage), I figure the change over shouldn't be that difficult. Here's what I figure I need:<P><OL TYPE=1><BR><LI> New accumulator<BR><LI> Get system vacuumed down<BR><LI> Recharge with R134<BR></OL><P>I know there are some deals about o-rings, but won't that problem be resolved with the new accumulator? If not, which o-rings do I need? And where do they get installed?<P>Thanks,<BR>Heis<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||wkayl||04-11-2001||12:30 AM||||Here is the best page I've seen on the subject..<BR> <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/FIEROWAREHOUSE/134aconversion.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/FIEROWAREHOUSE/134aconversion.html</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||04-11-2001||09:25 AM||||Replace the orifice tube too. This has a screen covering it that is supposed to catch debris in the system. You'll probably find that it's plugged. And they're cheap ($3 or so).||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroJoe||04-11-2001||10:10 AM||||If you were planning on recharging it yourself, you better read this topic:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009533.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum2/HTML/009533.html</A> <P>I had mine recharged professionally, very cold.  Cost about $130.<P>------------------<BR>AIM: Snowboarder0311<BR>87 Coupe 5sp. - big resto in progress<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000004||862M4inCA||04-11-2001||12:13 PM||||last summer my uncle did a few 134a conversions and did nothing aside from getting the kit with the 134a fittings, oil, and the 134a. He did the conversions on an '81 mustang, '92 mercury, and '89 honda accord. All three maintained all the stock equipment from seals to receiver dryer (none of them had an accumulator) and so far all is still good with air cold enough to freeze you out. The only procedure he performed was drawing a vacuum in the system and keeping the pump on for at least an hour, after that it was just introducing the oil and 134a into the system. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||voyagerspe||04-11-2001||01:57 PM||||I did mine yesterday.  New fittings, new oil and 3 cans of 134a.  My air is ICE cold I put a thermometor in the vent and its blowing at 38F.  I had no problems and I didnt replace the accumulator or orcife tube. I did let it vacumme down with a pump for over an hour.  Good luck<BR> ||||reg||14
